<Project>

  <!-- .NET for iOS does not resolve a native out of runtimes/<rid>/native/ the
       way a desktop app does, and a framework is a directory rather than a
       file, so there is nothing a payload layout could express. It has to be a
       <NativeReference>, which is a thing only the consuming project can
       declare - hence this file, injected into every project that references
       the package.

       Two slices, and they are not interchangeable: a simulator binary will
       not load on a device, and the reverse is worse because it links and then
       fails at runtime. RuntimeIdentifier is what tells them apart, and it is
       always set on an iOS build.

       The platform guard is not belt and braces. buildTransitive exists so
       this reaches an app through a shared library, and that library is built
       once per head - so a RuntimeIdentifier test alone is true for osx-arm64
       and android-arm64 as well, and would hand a macOS build an iOS
       framework. TargetPlatformIdentifier is what says which head this is.

       $(MSBuildThisFileDirectory) is buildTransitive/<tfm>/ inside the
       extracted package, so the frameworks sit two levels up under
       native/ - see the .csproj that packs them. -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <FFAudioFrameworks>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../../native/ios</FFAudioFrameworks>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(TargetPlatformIdentifier)' == 'ios' And $(RuntimeIdentifier.StartsWith('iossimulator'))">
    <NativeReference Include="$(FFAudioFrameworks)/ios-simulator/ffaudio.framework">
      <Kind>Framework</Kind>
    </NativeReference>
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(TargetPlatformIdentifier)' == 'ios' And '$(RuntimeIdentifier)' != '' And !$(RuntimeIdentifier.StartsWith('iossimulator'))">
    <NativeReference Include="$(FFAudioFrameworks)/ios-device/ffaudio.framework">
      <Kind>Framework</Kind>
    </NativeReference>
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
